  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Scattered showers and a few thunderstorms today, \nshowers continue into Saturday early afternoon. Gusty winds tonight \nthrough Saturday.\n\nA cold front continues to track eastward towards the area, currently \nacross central WV, and is triggering some scattered showers in that \narea. The front is fairly progressive, and looks to reach the area \nby 4 PM or so today. With some MLCAPE ahead of the system, and \ndecent warming today, especially in the Foothills and Piedmont, \ncannot rule out a few thunderstorms this afternoon, though the \nthreat for strong to severe storms is far to the north of the area. \n\nStrong warm air advection ahead of the front will push temperatures \nto the mid to upper 80s in the Piedmont. Plenty of sunshine still \neven in the west have allowed temperatures to already rise into the \nmid 70s, so still have time for a few more degrees of heating to \noccur before cloud cover and precipitation from the approaching \nfront arrives.\n\nThe 500mb trough will cross the area tonight, and so expect showers \nto linger during the overnight hours and through Saturday morning. \nAs the main piece of energy moves to the east by the afternoon, \nshowers will erode generally from west to east, leaving the rest of \nthe day dry. Rainfall amounts have increased, mainly for the \nPiedmont and VA Southside, so could now see up to a half of an inch \nof rain for those areas, and between two to three tenths of an inch \nelsewhere.\n\nWind gusts will continue to increase through the day, peaking during \nthe overnight hours tonight, and gradually diminishing by sunset \nSaturday, though turning from the southwest to the northwest \nfollowing the frontal passage later today. Gusts will be between 25 \nto 35 mph, locally higher along the ridges. Make sure to secure any \noutdoor Halloween decorations.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Frost in the mountains Sunday morning.\n\nConfidence has continued to increase on the potential for areas of \nfrost to develop in the mountains by Sunday morning, as high \npressure behind the front settles over the Mid Atlantic with \nclearing skies and calmer winds. Highest confidence is over the \nwesternmost counties in the mountains, from Greenbrier County WV to \nWatauga County NC, where look to fall to or just below freezing. The \nelevated valleys will be even more susceptible to frost, as the\natmosphere decouples overnight. A Freeze Watch has been issued \nfor those locations for late Saturday night through Sunday \nmorning. Take steps now to protect cold sensitive vegetation. \nNot as high confidence in frost farther east, especially along \nthe Blue Ridge, as there may be enough wind to keep the \natmosphere mixed enough to keep temperatures above freezing. \n\nSunday night into Monday morning will be cold as well, but not quite \nas cold. Still cold enough to warrant a heavier coat or an extra \nlayer or two while waiting at the bus stop. After that, temperatures \nstart to moderate towards normal for the rest of the week.\n\nEarly next week, models are coming into consensus on a piece of \nenergy aloft becoming cut off from the main trough as it tracks\ntowards the coast, and a surface low developing along the front\nonce it moves offshore; however, there is still plenty of \ndisagreement in the eventual positioning of the upper cut off \nlow. While any impacts will be mainly limited to the coasts, \nguidance has trended towards increasing rain chances for the \nPiedmont and Southside for the early part of next week.",
